  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1040 PM MDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nSandwiched between a trough in the Pacific Northwest and a \nridge over the Southern Plains, southwesterly flow is dominant \naloft over the Desert Southwest. This flow is advecting in a \nrich plume of tropical Pacific moisture, with PWATs near an inch\nacross the region. The one caveat to this deep moisture are the\nlingering dry surface layers. This evening's 00z KGJT sounding \nshows a pronounced inverted-V profile, with the column saturated\naloft but quite dry near the surface. As a result, most \nlocations have seen virga showers and strong, gusty winds this \nevening, rather than heavy rainfall. Areas in northeast Utah \nwhere showers have been training for the last 6 hours have \nfinally seen precipitation make it to the surface, but totals \nhave been limited to just a few hundredths. With plenty of \nmoisture but a lack of instability, this type of activity will \ncontinue through the night, with gusty winds and light rainfall \nthrough daybreak tomorrow. \n\nDrier air moves in after daybreak, bringing an end to any \nlingering showers and allowing skies to clear, setting the stage\nfor a drier week ahead. The Pacific Northwest trough tracks \nalong the Canadian border over the coming days, while high \npressure builds westward into the Desert Southwest, pushing a \nridge up along the West Coast. Drier zonal flow will be in \ncontrol over eastern Utah and western Colorado by midweek, \nallowing temperatures to warm up a few degrees, running around 5\ndegrees above normal. Enough surface moisture will linger to \nfuel a few isolated showers or thunderstorms over the higher \nterrain Monday and Tuesday, but chances dwindle by midweek.",
